1. What is Nginx?

Nginx (pronounced "Engine-X") is an open-source web server that is used to:

Host websites
Serve web pages (HTML, CSS, JavaScript, images)
Act as a Reverse Proxy
Load Balance traffic
Handle HTTPS (SSL/TLS)

Nginx is a software that receives requests from users' web browsers and sends back the requested website or application.

2. Real-Life Example

Imagine a restaurant.

👨 Customer = User using a web browser
🍽️ Waiter = Nginx
👨‍🍳 Kitchen = Website/Application

The customer asks for food.

The waiter takes the order to the kitchen.

The kitchen prepares the food.

The waiter brings the food back.

3. Why Do We Need Nginx?

Suppose you create a website.

You save this file:

index.html

But nobody on the Internet can see it.

You need a Web Server.

Nginx:

Receives requests
Finds the webpage
Sends it to the browser

Without a web server, the website cannot be accessed by users.

4. How Nginx Works

Suppose a user enters:

http://example.com
Step 1

Browser sends a request.

↓

Step 2

Nginx receives the request.

↓

Step 3

Nginx finds:

/var/www/html/index.html

↓

Step 4

Nginx sends the page back.

↓

Step 5

The browser displays the website.

5. Installing Nginx
Ubuntu
sudo apt update
sudo apt install nginx

RHEL / Rocky / AlmaLinux
sudo dnf install nginx

6. Start Nginx
sudo systemctl start nginx

7. Open the Website

Find your server IP.

ip addr

Suppose IP is

192.168.1.10

Open your browser.

http://192.168.1.10

You should see the Welcome to nginx! page.

8. Default Website Location

Ubuntu

/var/www/html

Example

cd /var/www/html

Create

sudo nano index.html

Write

<h1>Welcome to My Website</h1>

Save.

Refresh the browser.

You will see your webpage.

9. Restart Nginx

Whenever you change configuration files,

restart or reload Nginx.

Restart

sudo systemctl restart nginx

Reload (applies configuration changes without stopping active connections)

sudo systemctl reload nginx

10. Nginx Logs

Access Log

/var/log/nginx/access.log

Shows every request made to the server.

11. Error Log

/var/log/nginx/error.log

12. Reverse Proxy

One of the most common uses of Nginx.

Suppose your application runs on:

http://localhost:3000

Instead of users accessing port 3000,

Nginx receives requests on port 80 or 443 and forwards them to the application.

Flow

Browser

↓

Nginx

↓

Application (Port 3000)

The user never needs to know the application's internal port.

13.Load Balancing

Suppose your website has three servers.

Server1

Server2

Server3

Instead of sending all users to one server,

Nginx distributes requests.

Users

↓

Nginx

↓

Server1

Server2

Server3

This improves performance and availability.

14. HTTPS Support

Nginx can use SSL/TLS certificates.

Instead of

http://example.com

users access

https://example.com

Benefits:

Encrypts data between the browser and the server.
Protects passwords and sensitive information.
Verifies the server's identity.
